  "id": "Compound::DB00264 - Gene::153",
  "verbalization": "Metoprolol is linked to Adrb1 through a series of molecular and pharmacological interactions that collectively suggest a role in beta-adrenergic receptor modulation. The evidence indicates that Metoprolol, as a beta-adrenergic antagonist, causes dermatitis psoriasiform and retroperitoneal fibrosis, both of which are associated with Atenolol and Sotalol, respectively. These drugs bind to Adrb1, suggesting a potential mechanism by which Metoprolol influences receptor activity. The shared ancestry of Metoprolol with Atenolol and Sotalol, both belonging to the beta-adrenergic antagonist class, underscores a common pharmacological pathway. The integration of these findings implies that Metoprolol may exert its effects through a mechanism involving the beta-adrenergic system, with Adrb1 being a key target. The evidence supports a direct relationship between Metoprolol and Adrb1, mediated through the beta-adrenergic pathway, and highlights the importance of receptor binding in the drug's pharmacological profile.",
